{"name":"Honey Locust","hex":"#ffc367","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"honey locust","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A North American deciduous tree (Gleditsia triacanthos) having fragrant flowers, hard red wood, and conspicuous thorns."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/honey_locust"}],"entries":[{"title":"Honey locust","blocks":[{"text":"The honey locust, also known as the thorny locust or thorny honeylocust, is a deciduous tree in the family Fabaceae, native to central North America where it is mostly found in the moist soil of river valleys. Honey locust trees are highly adaptable to different environments, and the species has been introduced worldwide. Outside its natural range it can be an aggressive, damaging invasive species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Gleditsia_triacanthos_%28Honeylocust%29_%2838246396371%29.jpg/330px-Gleditsia_triacanthos_%28Honeylocust%29_%2838246396371%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey_locust","wikidata":"Q157650"},{"title":"Honey","blocks":[{"text":"Honey is a sweet and viscous substance made by several species of bees, the best-known of which are honey bees. Honey is made and stored to nourish bee colonies. Bees produce honey by gathering and then refining the sugary secretions of plants or the secretions of other insects, like the honeydew of aphids. This refinement takes place both within individual bees, through regurgitation and enzymatic activity, and during storage in the hive, through water evaporation that concentrates the honey's sugars until it is thick and viscous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Runny_hunny.jpg/330px-Runny_hunny.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey","wikidata":"Q10987"},{"title":"Locust","blocks":[{"text":"Locusts are various species of short-horned grasshoppers in the family Acrididae that have a swarming phase. These insects are usually solitary, but under certain circumstances they become more abundant and change their behaviour and habits, becoming gregarious. No taxonomic distinction is made between locust and grasshopper species; the basis for the definition is whether a species forms swarms under intermittently suitable conditions. Swarming has evolved independently in multiple lineages, comprising at least 18 genera in 5 different subfamilies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Locusta-migratoria-wanderheuschrecke.jpg/330px-Locusta-migratoria-wanderheuschrecke.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Locust","wikidata":"Q83931"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/HoneyLocustThorn.JPG/250px-HoneyLocustThorn.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/HoneyLocustThorn.JPG/330px-HoneyLocustThorn.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/HoneyLocustThorn.JPG/250px-HoneyLocustThorn.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHoneyLocustThorn.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Honey_Locust_Tree_Thorns_in_Kansas.jpg/250px-Honey_Locust_Tree_Thorns_in_Kansas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Honey_Locust_Tree_Thorns_in_Kansas.jpg/500px-Honey_Locust_Tree_Thorns_in_Kansas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Honey_Locust_Tre